 "intro": "Carthage, 147-146 BC, in the Third Punic War. Rome has resolved to destroy its old enemy, and the siege has dragged on for two years. You are Publius Cornelius Scipio Aemilianus, consul at 37.",
 "debrief": "In Appian’s account, drawn from Polybius, who was there, Scipio restored discipline, walled off the isthmus and built a mole across the harbour mouth. The Carthaginians dug a new channel and launched a fleet, which was beaten. In spring 146 BC the Romans broke into the city and fought for six days through the streets to the citadel of Byrsa, where 50,000 people surrendered. Hasdrubal begged for his life; his wife killed herself and her children in the burning temple. The city was destroyed. Scipio is said to have wept and quoted Homer on the fall of Troy. The other choices are what-ifs.",
